hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  any book one on Duty's Cicero is going  to explore the virtue of justice in a  very important way now on duties is  Cicero's presentation of stoic ethics in  particular ethics in terms of the four  cardinal virtues and quite often when  people are approaching these four  cardinal virtues as wisdom justice  courage and temperance they think that's  something that's been left out and the  stories actually have some very  important clarifications of that and  this indeed is one of those very  important clarifications because the  sister was going to tell us that justice  doesn't just include what we might call  strict justice it also includes what we  can translate as beneficence or as you  might say a different kind of goodness  that falls under justice beneficence is  not a word that we typically use it's  actually it's just a transliteration of  the Latin benefit Kennedy which means  doing good to other people so we might  call it in another old fashioned word  charity Cicero actually does use the  word Caritas at certain points he more  often uses these other terms which we'll  discuss shortly but the general idea is  that justice as a virtue is something  wider something more encompassing than  merely following the rules or fulfilling  you know what is do you know following  the roles and expectations that you have  it includes the things that we would  term you know going beyond giving  engaging in charity work or volunteer  work all of those can fall under  beneficence so let's start where Cicero  actually begins this he talks about  justice as a virtue that is  good character trait something that we  want to emulate something that we want  to see in ourselves and others and like  each of the virtues justice has its very  basis in human nature and it's something  that we as the kind of animals that we  are that as rational animals are you  might say designed or intended in some  way to have as part of the way in which  we comport ourselves so it depends on  some inclinations that are implanted  within the very core of human nature  very often not realized sometimes only  realized partly all of that is quite  true but all of these inclinations are  there and Cicero says that one of the  major inclinations that we have is what  we could call the social instinct we  want to gather together with in  functioning groups and societies and we  need justice in order to do that well so  at one point he talks about justice as  maintaining human society that's just  you know so Keith Haas and a community  or as the translation here it has common  bonds of life communitas vitae that is a  life that we live together not as  isolated individuals but as individuals  who are in relation to each other in an  ongoing way of common life now this is  actually going to encompass something  quite wide and in the end one of the  things that he does talk about those  dogs were Cosmopolitan's this is this in  theory encompasses the entire human race  but generally we think about things that  are closer to us the family our friends  our local community our our nation or  our state and he tells us in in  consequence of this that justice  actually has two parts or that is virtue  has two parts  then he says is called justice he says  there are two divisions justice which is  the crowning glory of the virtues and on  the basis of which men are called good  men and then he says clothes akin  conn-young to that is like conjoined -  it's actually one of the words that is  used for talking about spouses can join  with justice is beneficence benefi Ken  TIA  he says which may also be titled  kindness for generosity so each of these  is really part of justice real ours  there's justice in a strict sense which  includes a lot of different things I'm  not going to try to tell you all of it  here but here are some examples of what  Cicero places under other than that  so giving others what is due to them  giving others what they deserve  you know distributing offices or  resources or even punishments or or  detriments fairly that's one notice of  justice he very much stresses not  harming other people not allowing others  to come to harm when we can prevent it  as also being part of justice and then a  third really important thing is when we  make commitments or promises or incur  obligations that we actually put a fill  them and that is justice in the strict  sense he also includes many other things  under that same sort of rubric you know  for example talking about how we want to  treat the enemy in wartime the Stoics  did believe in something like a Just War  theory that would fall under strict  justice we need more than that that's  not enough to have the entire virtue  that allows us to live together well in  human society this is a problem that  gets discussed by many moral theorists  and you know you could say something  like this well so long as I don't  transgress anybody else's rights and I  keep my  and other stuff what does it matter if  I'm nice to them what does it matter if  I speak to them what does it matter if I  have you know invite them to my backyard  barbecues what does it matter if I  exchange things with them what does it  matter if I pitch in when they're having  some trouble that that's all besides the  point I don't have to do that sort of  thing  Cicero and the Stoics would say well if  that's your attitude you're not really  just because you're missing this other  half of the virtue of justice there's  justice in the strict sense that's very  good don't skimp on it  but there's also beneficence to be  magnificent well let's look at some of  the things that he says about it he says  we can also call this kindness one of  the words that he uses over and over  again is bending but making a pass which  means being which is the word we get  benign from being good to others giving  them things helping them out generosity  liberally toss this is another key idea  that in some ethics will be separated  off as a separate virtue Cicero is  placing that under justice under  beneficence he uses other terms like you  talk to us about friendship i'ma keep  TIA  he talks about love for those within  your family you know more he talks about  what we might call you know giving  kavita's or affection he also talks  about benevolence which is just a  transliteration of that Evelyn TIA which  is very similar to beneficence  benevolence stresses the attitude the  wheeling part beneficent stresses the  actually doing something part so you  could be benevolent in a certain sense  but never actually  you weren't able to distribute any goods  to other people so this covers a wide  range this might have to do with how you  give people money it might include what  you do with your time what you do with  your attention whether you in fact  treat people well whether you have a  kind word or a smile for somebody who's  down all of that can fall under  beneficence the other thing that's very  important to stress about this is that  Cicero does not think that we have you  might call a sort of unlimited universal  requirement of beneficence he stresses  that in fact we we can't be beneficent  towards everybody except in a very  limited way we might in fact have to  choose between you know different people  who we give our rare resources to or who  we spend our time with and he thinks  that there are some some general or  basic rules or principles that we can  use to understand how we ought to  apportion this so beneficence and  justice actually connect up with each  other in another way because justice  informs beneficence beneficence goes  beyond what strict justice requires but  it's also still going to be governed to  some extent by strict justice  particularly giving others what is due  to them so these two together comprise  the actual virtue of justice for Cicero  and we don't want to leave this part out  if we do leave this part out then we're  not really just in the sense that the  Stoics talked about so he devotes a lot  of attention to discussing the ins and  outs of beneficence another thing to  keep in mind is that you know this is  much more likely to be part of what we  could call our personal life we should  be just in the strict sense in our  person  life but this is where we have the realm  of our relationships that play  themselves out so both of these are  going to be about equally important in  realizing this social inclination or  instinct whatever you want to call it  and perfecting it rationally making it  into a virtue  you
 

